+## 2. Scheduled-CI triage correction — the "revert to last green" premise is invalid
+
+#2738 §Recovery names the fix path: *"triage CI run 40653481018 / commit `feda45e4` on `supremeai-core` (fix-forward or revert to last green `832b27e6`), then admin HITL redeploy."* Live triage of the complete Actions history (49/49 runs fetched via tower `github.workflow_runs`, 2026-10-30) **corrects this path**:
+
+### 2.1 The scheduled lane has never been green
+
+All 5 scheduled invocations of the `ci` workflow (the only events that run the "🔧 PyErrorFix Engine" job, per its `if:` guard) are failures — **0/5 scheduled successes**:
+
+| run # | event | head sha | conclusion | date | run id |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 41 | schedule | feda45e4 | failure | 2026-10-29T08:06Z | 40839818660 |
+| 40 | schedule | feda45e4 | failure | 2026-10-28T07:58Z | 40653481018 |
+| 36 | schedule | 419a8388 | failure | 2026-10-27T07:23Z | 40466830817 |
+| 26 | schedule | cf11b9cc | failure | 2026-10-26T07:28Z | 40286008931 |
+| 24 | schedule | f2a47fc5 | failure | 2026-10-25T07:19Z | 40172351812 |
+
+### 2.2 Same commits: green on push, red on schedule
+
+Every commit that carries both a push run and a scheduled run is **green on push, red on schedule** — the scheduled failure is commit-independent:
+
+- `cf11b9cc`: push #25 **success** ↔ schedule #26 **failure**
+- `f2a47fc5`: push #23 **success** ↔ schedule #24 **failure**
+- `419a8388`: push #35 **success** ↔ schedule #36 **failure**
+
+### 2.3 feda45e4 never had a push-triggered CI run at all
+
+In the complete 49-run history, `feda45e4` appears in exactly 2 runs — both scheduled, both failed (#40, #41). There is no push-run for this commit, so the "commit `feda45e4` broke CI" inference rested solely on scheduled failures that are now shown to be chronic. "Revert to last green `832b27e6`" would **not** turn the scheduled lane green (its own scheduled run #36-era commits were equally red), and "fix-forward" aimed at `feda45e4` addresses a regression that this evidence shows does not exist.
+
+### 2.4 Structural step analysis — which step can actually fail
+
+The workflow (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, 5,422 bytes, byte-size identical at `f2a47fc5` and current main) makes almost the whole PyErrorFix Engine job unfailable:
+
+- `Run pyerrorfix (detect + auto-fix + SARIF)`: both `pyerrorfix analyze` module invocations (launched via the interpreter's `-m` flag against the target tree) end with a shell error-swallowing fallback (`|| true`); even a missing/invalid SARIF is replaced by an empty-valid stub — this step cannot fail the job.
+- `Trigger Federation CI Watchdog on Failure`: `curl … || true` — cannot fail.
+- `Upload SARIF to GitHub code scanning` (`github/codeql-action/upload-sarif@v4`, `if: always()`) is the **only step in the job that can produce a failure conclusion**.
+
+All three sampled scheduled runs (#24, #36, #41) end in an identical log tail: checkout/teardown + the Node-20 deprecation warning naming `github/codeql-action/upload-sarif` — consistent with the upload step being the terminal failing step. **Honest limitation:** the tower's `github.get_failed_logs` returns only a ~2.1 KB tail that ends at the deprecation warning; the actual error line of the failing step is not visible from the tower vantage (public GitHub API is rate-limited 403 from this sandbox — same limitation #2738 already records). The "only the SARIF upload can fail" conclusion is therefore structural (derived from the workflow definition's `|| true` / `if: always()` wiring), tail-corroborated, not error-line-proven. Typical root causes for a chronically failing `upload-sarif` on a private repo are plan/permission-shaped (code scanning unavailable or `security-events: write` rejected) — adjudicating that choice (gate the step with `continue-on-error` / visibility condition, vs. enabling code scanning) is an admin-lane decision and is deliberately NOT patched here.
+
+### 2.5 Consequence for the recovery path
+
+The #2225 unblock sequence needs one correction: before or alongside admin HITL redeploy, the **scheduled lane itself must be repaired** (its chronic red is what the CI Guardian/federation watchdog sees on the core repo), and that repair is a workflow-definition change in `.github/` — protected-path/admin-review territory, out of the external-agent write lane. Reverting source to `832b27e6` is not a recovery action for this failure mode.
